WEBVTT

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:07.970
From the sands of the Arabian desert rises the tallest man-made structure on Earth.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.910
A city in the sky, nearly half a kilometer tall.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.100
The Borg Dubai is the pinnacle of skyscraper engineering.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:29.030
It owes its success to seven key inventions.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.320
We have lined up seven landmark buildings,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.600
historic giants of the skyscraper world.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.360
At the heart of each one lies a major technological innovation

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.140
that allowed engineers to reach ever taller into the sky.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.490
One by one, traveling up the scale,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.560
we'll reveal the incredible stories

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.970
behind these structures and the inv...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.920
that have driven them higher.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:09.000
Seven ingenious leaps forward that have enabled skyscrapers to evolve

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.610
from big to bigger into the world's biggest.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.670
Dubai is the fastest growing city in the world.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.760
An army of cranes and laborers work around the clock

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.440
to turn the city into a place that the whole world will look up to.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:09.440
The centerpiece in the grand plan is the Burj Dubai, the tallest skyscraper in the world.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.210
It is the ultimate leap in a series of historic engineering breakthroughs.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.100
how the bourgeois Dubai can be this tall we need to go back in time and look at how

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:41.980
skyscrapers begin the first breakthrough happens in the 19th century with a

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:48.220
building that's only 43 meters tall the builders of the equitable life building

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.630
in New York realize that before buildings can reach taller they must

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.200
find a way to make people climb higher

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:04.470
The first big obstacle to skyscrapers is the stair.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.590
People weren't willing to walk up lots of flights of stairs.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:12.710
Stairs in old office buildings were long, they were very dark,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:18.080
and people weren't willing to climb stairs more than to the second or the third floor.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.820
And if you were a lawyer and you were looking for clients and you were on the fifth floor,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.540
you were undoubtedly going to lose clients because they didn't want to come up to your office.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.560
By the time you reach the sixth floor...

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.110
Seventh Floor, you're incredibly winded.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.240
That's it for me.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.230
There's an obvious solution to the problem.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:45.870
The elevator.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.870
Early elevators have one deadly fault.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.940
Nothing stops...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.820
than falling if the rope breaks.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.990
But then a mechanic from Vermont invents a device

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.390
that can stop a falling elevator almost instant.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:22.230
In 1854, Elisha Graves Otis demonstrates his invention

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.990
at the World's Fair in New York.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.660
He stands on a platform high up in the air,

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:29.970
apparently held only by a man.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.160
All right, Stan, be gentle with me.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.350
Then Otis tells his gasping audience

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.960
that the rope is about to be cut.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.340
Stan, cut the rope.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:54.100
And at the end of that cutting of the rope,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.580
he reportedly took off his top hat, bowed to the audience

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.970
and said, all s—

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.900
All safe gentlemen, all safe.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:07.960
This is the world's first fully automated safety elevator.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.860
It's a simple yet very clever invention.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.050
The critical element, the elevator rope,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:22.530
is secured with a powerful wagon spring mounted on top of the cab.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:28.280
This spring connects to a set of metal prongs on each side of the elevator.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:29.860
The prongs...

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.860
run along guide rails fitted with a row of teeth.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:37.590
When the rope breaks, it triggers a chain of events.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:42.370
The spring relaxes and forces the metal prongs

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.410
into the teeth, locking the cab in place.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.740
This is a true engineering breakthrough,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.260
a fact lost on Elisha Otis.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.380
He was not a businessman, he was not a marketer,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.510
he was a mechanic, he was a tinkerer.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.990
And I don't know that he fully recognized the power of this invention.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:15.630
The elevator completely transforms the urban landscape,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:23.590
and it all starts on a busy street corner in downtown New York.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:29.530
It was the site of the Equitable Life Assurance Company's headquarters.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.960
of extraordinary significance

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.040
because it was the first office building with an elevator.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.740
And without an elevator, the skyscraper would be impossible.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:40.910
The building standing here today

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:43.290
is the new Equitable Life headquarters.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.800
It has replaced the old Equitable Life building,

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:54.730
which was not a skyscraper as we know it.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:58.910
It didn't scrape the sky, it didn't soar up,

00:06:59.320 --> 00:06:59.980
but it...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.460
change the notion of what an office building could be.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:08.630
Until the Equitable life, the lower floors of a building are the most desirable, but

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:12.870
the elevator completely reverses the economics of real estate.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:19.690
Equitable understood that the upper floors now, which received light and air and were

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:23.940
away from the noise of traffic, would be the most valuable spaces in the building.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:24.920
So what did they do?

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.900
They occupied the lowest stories in the building and they rented out at the highest rents possible.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:36.160
the upper floors of the building so it's the whole idea of a skyscraper with high rents and

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.900
views and light and air on the upper floors which we still deal with today that was invented at the

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:52.590
equitable building the bourgeois dubai takes the idea of the elevator to the extreme equitable life

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:59.840
We've had seven and a half stories, the Borge Dubai has over 160, a height that stretches

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.140
which stretches elevator technology to its absolute limit.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:23.460
the Borge Dubai will be able to accommodate 35,000 people.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.960
Getting a population the size of a small town in and out of

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.060
The building is the ultimate challenge for the elevators.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:41.300
To cope with the numbers, the Borg Dubai has 53 different elevators.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:49.080
Some reach over 35 kilometers an hour and climb 120 floors in under 50 seconds.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.390
The biggest lifts carry up to 46 passengers.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.000
Stopping such.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.040
Such a speeding juggernaut in an emergency is a titanic challenge.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:09.310
We're now talking 30 tons, 40 tons, 50 tons of moving mass.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:11.360
what happens to an elevator.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:16.910
brake system is the equivalent of driving an 18-wheel truck.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.100
off a cliff.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.930
and stopping it in midair.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:26.360
As soon as an elevator on the Borscht Dubai exceeds its speed limit,

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.200
emergency brakes spring into action.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:37.760
Metal brake shoes bite down on the guide rails and generate enough braking power to stop the elevator within a few meters.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:42.840
But at the end of the day,

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:44.390
the safety.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.100
that we depend on.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.600
that the riders depend on to bring the car to a safe

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.500
And the controlled stop is still.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:53.340
Thank you.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:54.520
a mechanical device.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.560
All safe, gentlemen.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:02.920
All safe.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:04.490
Thank you.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.960
The safety elevator allows the skyscraper to break through the five-story barrier.

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:18.050
suddenly

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.550
tall buildings a big business.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.130
But as they approach 80 meters,

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:28.160
Traditional building materials are no longer strong enough.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:34.490
To make the leap to the 87-meter Fuller Flatiron Building in New York,

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.420
The skyscraper must be reinvented.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:46.740
This is the Monadnok building.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:47.650
in Chicago.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.020
a living fossil in the skyscraper world.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:00.000
When it opens in 1893, it's the world's largest office block.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:03.940
but its 16 stories stretch stone to the limit.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:09.800
The walls at the bottom must be a whopping two metres thick

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.760
to bear the weight of the monadna.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:13.780
The structure.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.000
is so extremely heavy.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.510
that it begins to sink into the soft Chicago soil.

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:24.690
Eventually,

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.300
half a meter of bricks and mortar.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:28.920
disappear underground.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:34.180
Obviously.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:35.560
stone.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:37.560
is not skyscraper material.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.770
So when the architect of the Monadnock

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.050
Daniel Burnham.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.600
plans the Fuller building in New York.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:51.100
He's in a bit of a pickle.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:57.320
The extremely narrow plot dictates that his 22-story skyscraper

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:58.800
has to be triangular.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.990
Burnham knows that there is no room for stone walls.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:12.160
They would have to be so thick that there would be hardly any space left on the ground floor.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:19.410
And wasting valuable space is a cardinal sin for a skyscraper architect.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:24.500
Stone is out of the question.

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:29.970
But if you...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.920
Look at the Fuller flat iron today.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.360
It looks like it's built from stone.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:36.790
So how did Burnham do it?

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:39.360
It's all just a facade.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:45.380
Under the skin of the Fuller

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:49.420
lies one of the most important building technologies ever invented.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:55.120
Burnham makes the building out of steel columns and steel beams

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.860
locked together into a steel skeleton.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.060
Steel is much stronger than stone, so this skeleton can be thin and light, yet it can

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.320
support the weight of the whole structure.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:15.710
To keep the weather out, Burnham can simply hang thin masonry walls off the steel frame

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:17.170
like curtains.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:23.650
The building is an immediate success.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:29.430
The flat iron building was the first skyscraper to become a symbol of New York.

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:37.150
Attracted photographers, famous painters painted the building, and it became a symbol at the highest levels of culture.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.180
The flat iron even affects the weather.

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:53.350
The shape of the building caused a wind tunnel effect, and men used to hang out at the corner and watch for women's dresses to blow up so that they could get a look at women's ankles.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:58.850
Steel construction truly moves the skyscraper on.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.100
This is a new breed of building.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.360
Once the steel skeleton frame is perfected,

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.540
basically you could build hypothetically to any height.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.680
And that meant that land values in commercial areas of New York

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:14.280
absolutely skyrocketed.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:18.320
Because no longer was land valued for an eight or 10

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:20.880
or 12 story building, which was the most you could build

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.460
without the steel skeleton frame.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.660
But now the sky was literally the limit.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.850
The skeleton of the Bourges de Baye combines the best of steel and stone.

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:49.840
It uses over 30,000 tons of steel, but in a very clever way.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:55.060
The steel is embedded in artificial stone, concrete.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.530
reinforced concrete backbone will be clad in a high-tech curtain wall of glass and steel.

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:16.310
The wall latches onto the Borge Dubai in units of up to two stories tall. The panels themselves

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:23.660
are rigid, but the joints between them are flexible. If an occupant moves a heavy piece

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:29.960
of furniture towards the edge of the skyscraper, the floor will bend and force down the exterior.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:39.840
But the flexible joint in between the wall panels absorbs the movement, so the wall as a whole is not damaged.

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:48.940
The joints also allow each wall section to expand and contract as the desert sun passes around the skyscraper, heating it up.

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:54.090
But the most potent force it must withstand is the desert wind.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:59.980
The curtain wall of the Borge Dubai will cost $100.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.820
million dollars so before it's bolted on the engineers take prototype sections

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:15.340
for a test drive this is where the rubber meets the road today the

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:20.640
engineers apply the ultimate test an aircraft engine simulates a desert

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:27.480
storm we try to put everything that mother nature would throw out the

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.410
building into these tests if the brackets

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.080
just the wind loads, you have the prospect of the curtain wall peeling off the whole building.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:38.290
If you erected a curtain wall and you didn't find out it had an inherent design flaw,

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:43.160
like it leaked or something, until after it was occupied and the first storm came, it's unthinkable.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:55.130
The propeller spits wind and rain backwards of the curtain wall section mounted behind it.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:59.980
It reaches speeds of 75 kilometers an hour, a real challenge.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:05.000
the curtain wall. Will the prototype withstand the pressure?

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:15.190
Buffeting and the movement of the water across the surface is finding weaknesses and if there's

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:20.070
any flaws in connections or holes in the curtain wall the water will seep through. So if you see

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:27.740
any water coming through it's a fail and you know what's happening. But today the prototype passes

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:29.480
This is the test with flying colors.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:34.230
The Borge Dubai takes one step further to completion.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:39.960
Now the challenge for the engineers is to stop the baking desert sun

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:43.840
from turning their beautiful glass tower into a giant oven.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:51.960
Steel catapults skyscrapers to unseen heights.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:55.760
As walls now no longer have to bear all the weight,

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:59.110
architects can make them out of completely new materials.

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:10.880
Glass promises to flood buildings with light, but also with heat.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.920
To beat this new enemy, skyscrapers would need to get cooler.

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:27.250
When the United Nations design their new headquarters in New York, they face a dilemma.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.440
They want to cover the building with glass to make the interior as bright as possible.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:38.760
What they don't want is a 39-story greenhouse.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:50.980
A glass wall would allow a lot of light into the building, but also solar radiation.

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:57.620
This would be absorbed by the objects inside.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:18:59.980
These in turn...

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:04.660
radiate heat into the surrounding air and warm it up.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:09.990
As the sealed glass windows can't let the hot air escape,

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:13.240
things would get uncomfortable very quickly.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.620
You would basically be in an oven in the summer,

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:19.640
so you need to have artificial cooling

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.670
in order to make these glass skyscrapers work.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.460
An American engineer called Willis Carrier

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.190
cracks the cooling problem.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:36.180
He invents a machine that can cool and dry hot, moist air by making it wet.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:41.830
First, it injects a fine mist of cold water into a chamber.

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:47.280
Next, it sucks hot, soggy air into this cold mist.

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:51.250
Seen up close, something remarkable happens.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:55.870
As the hot air hits the surface of the cold water,

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:59.960
the air cools down and releases its moisture onto the...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:12.630
droplets so the air is cooled and dried in a single step once you have air

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:16.890
conditioning you no longer needed to be near windows so suddenly you could build

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:21.720
skyscrapers with enormous floor plates and people would be working on an

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:26.600
interior far away from a window and so air conditioning allowed for these

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:28.740
buildings that are really really massive

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.400
So all of these things together made the building pay to its maximum.

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:45.150
Air conditioning allows skyscrapers like the Borg Dubai to rise up in even the hottest climates.

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:50.860
And there is hardly a place on Earth where air conditioning matters more than Dubai.

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:55.360
Temperatures easily reach 40 degrees Celsius in the shade.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:58.580
The average humidity is 90 percent.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:20:59.980
A truly...

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:02.400
extreme environment for a skyscraper.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:07.740
The key to shielding the Burj Dubai from the brutal desert sun

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:10.070
is built into its glass skin.

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:12.990
The air conditioning couldn't keep up,

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:14.370
but we just used normal glass.

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:16.390
Well, this is an actual piece of the glass

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:17.610
that we're using on the building.

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:20.620
We have an outside face, an inside face.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:24.140
So what the outside coating does is reflects the daily solar

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:25.860
heat that comes direct from the sun

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:27.980
would pass into an apartment or office.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:34.160
The outside pane is coated with a thin layer of metal.

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:40.120
Like sunscreen, the metal coating deflects ultraviolet radiation

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.220
that would otherwise heat up the building.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:46.520
But the sunscreen is useless against the infrared

00:21:46.520 --> 00:21:49.160
that radiates from the hot desert sand.

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:53.550
So the inner pane is coated with a thin layer of silver

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:55.270
that keeps the heat rays out.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:59.980
Over 30,000 glass panels, enough to cover...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:05.190
The 2017 football fields will protect the Burj Dubai from the scorching heat.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:15.320
Air conditioning allows ever more people to work more comfortably inside skyscrapers.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:21.880
Taller skyscrapers, housing more workers, offer greater profits.

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:28.270
The only problem is that bigger skyscrapers take much longer to build.

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:37.710
To reach the dizzying 417 meters of the World Trade Center,

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:43.190
engineers have to invent a new, much faster way of building skyscrapers.

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:53.470
Long before the Twin Towers in New York become the tallest buildings in the world,

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:56.520
their developers face a mammoth problem.

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:07.800
The minute they start construction, the clock starts ticking.

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:12.740
Every day their building is unfinished costs them dearly.

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:18.470
So they have to work out how to reduce the construction time to the absolute minimum.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:25.330
The solution they come up with is to prefabricate sections of the towers

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:28.770
and assemble them like a giant jigsaw puzzle.

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:44.050
They build the sections off-site and ship them to the tower's construction site precisely when they are needed.

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:52.600
The only problem is how to lift the super-heavy 50-ton sections into place quickly enough.

00:23:57.710 --> 00:23:59.970
The traditional tool to build skyscrapers...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.500
like the Empire State Building is the Derrick Crane.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:07.820
But to get Derricks from one floor to the next,

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:12.520
they have to be disassembled, carried up, and reassembled,

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:15.200
a process that can take two days.

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:21.970
Such a crane is not going to be fast enough

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:23.670
for the World Trade Center.

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:26.410
These buildings were higher than anything

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:29.460
that had been constructed in the past, so.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.100
Naturally, we cast our eyes about the world to see what was there

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:36.120
and what might be better suited to the project.

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:40.280
The team find a revolutionary crane in Australia.

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:46.810
It can lift 50 tons, and four of them can reach into every corner of a twin tower.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:54.340
And once they have assembled three floors, an amazing thing happens.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:59.300
The bottom of the crane releases glimpses.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.720
slides up three stories and locks back into place.

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:12.240
And then the whole crane jumps itself up to the next level.

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:16.460
That's why it's called the Kangaroo Crane.

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:19.920
They were fast.

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:22.540
They had some defects.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:24.760
They spread a lot of oil around,

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:27.980
so it was a constant cleanup operation after them

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:29.980
and so forth, but they were fast.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:43.800
and quite reliable with the help of the prefabricated sections and the jumping kangaroo cranes the twin

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:55.830
towers take shape rapidly the builders managed to finish up to two floors every week in 1970

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:59.970
world trade tower one becomes the tallest building in the world

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:05.760
world, and while even more floors appear at the top, tenants move in at the bottom.

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:08.790
Time is money.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:27.700
On the Burj Dubai, the kangaroo crane is still the crane of choice.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:34.360
Here the builders have taken prefabrication to a new level.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:41.980
The key to speed is a new technology called jump forming.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.240
The process starts at the bottom of the building.

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:49.490
Steel workers assemble steel cages

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:51.130
that will become the backbone

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:53.430
for the floors and walls of the Borg Dubai.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:59.300
The kangaroo cranes hoist the steel cages up

00:26:59.680 --> 00:26:59.980
and...

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.940
Slot them into special moulds called jump forms.

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:12.680
In goes the concrete, and 12 hours later, when the concrete hardens, the form gets ready for the jump.

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:21.480
Hydraulic pistons push the form up, leaving the concrete block behind.

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:28.620
It takes only two hours for the form to move up to the next level, where the process starts all over again.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:37.040
This way, the Borge du Pai is cast in place, layer by layer, like a giant wedding cake.

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:45.380
But delivering concrete to the top of the tower gets more difficult with every floor.

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:58.770
Five o'clock, and the men on the day shift go home.

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:13.420
But on the top of the Borge Dubai, the casting crew are waiting for the concrete to arrive.

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:18.460
What we're standing on right now will be the 155th floor.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:21.340
So we're going to pour some walls tonight.

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:25.030
Below, workers prepare the pumps.

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:29.980
They can really only pour concrete at 9...

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.620
because in the searing daytime temperatures the concrete would

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:45.940
overheat. They need 630 horsepower pumps to cope with the 25 tons of concrete

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:51.660
contained in each pipe and they're about to pump this concrete higher than anyone

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:57.440
has ever done before. An amazing challenge for the pumping system.

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:09.360
We're pumping 570 metres at the moment, which is a world record for a building site.

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:15.020
It's a very aggressive environment within the pipe, with the high pressures, the aggregates,

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.680
rubbing against the steel, you've got to watch out because you can wear through the steel

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.140
and the pipes first.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:29.980
It takes 40 minutes for the concrete to travel up the pipes from the bottom to the 155th.

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:47.010
floor it's a combined effort of raw machine power and subtle chemistry if the

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:53.560
concrete is too thin and set slowly it causes delays if it's too thick it may

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:59.870
set too soon and block the pipes that night it takes

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.540
until 4 a.m. before the job is finished.

00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:06.640
Now the concrete and steel skeleton of the Borsh Dubai

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:08.400
is nearly complete.

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:12.760
On a mammoth project that will cost about $1 billion

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:15.630
to build, every day is precious.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:25.100
But the system has worked to perfection.

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:29.920
The tower is nearly 600 meters tall, and a new floor

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:43.580
goes on every three days. Prefabrication technology allows giant skyscrapers to grow

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:52.190
even faster, which makes them even more profitable and desirable. But as skyscrapers soar even higher

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:59.900
into the clouds, they become exposed to a new enemy, one that exploits every weakness, the wind.

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:06.880
To build the 442-meter Sears Tower in Chicago, the proverbial windy city,

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.760
engineers must turn the skyscraper inside out.

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:23.880
In 1970, the architects building the new headquarters for Sears and Roebuck in Chicago face a problem.

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:29.900
Their skyscraper, the Sears Tower, will be over 100 floors tall.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.530
a height that exposes it to huge wind forces.

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:39.000
Building this skyscraper using a traditional steel skeleton

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:40.840
would cause massive problems.

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:47.600
The taller a steel skeleton gets, the more susceptible it is to bending in high winds.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:53.920
Gusts off Lake Michigan can buffet a skyscraper at up to 80 kilometers an hour.

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:58.960
This causes the upper floors to sway, affecting the workers inside.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.320
The motion of a tall building is more like the motion of a ship.

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:04.880
It creates a kind of seasickness.

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:08.240
There's that same sense in a very tall building.

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:11.920
So it's necessary to reduce the swaying component down

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:12.780
so that people...

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:13.700
are not sick.

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:18.680
The architects of the Sears Tower

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.780
Invent a technology that can beat the wind.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:26.210
they shift the steel framework.

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:28.140
from the inside of a building.

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:29.970
to the outcome.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:30.380
science.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.360
This so-called exoskeleton...

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:37.420
makes it very hard for wind to bend the building.

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:42.630
in the Sears Tower.

00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:47.410
Nine such tubes locked together to make the building rock solid.

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:56.560
The exoskeleton.

00:32:56.910 --> 00:32:59.950
is the best way of resisting wind ever invented.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:08.990
Even at wind speeds of over 90 kilometers per hour, the top floor of the Sears Tower only moves 15 centimeters.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:16.680
Bourges Dubai is expected to be nearly twice as tall as the Sears Tower.

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:23.040
At this extreme height, fighting the wind with a rigid exoskeleton is not good enough.

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:29.960
To stop the high caliber residents from getting seasick, the architects turn to highly...

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:31.620
advanced aerodynamics.

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:36.270
The most important thing in a tall building is the way it interacts with the wind.

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:40.810
And so what we did is we essentially, as we designed this building, we kept testing it in the wind tunnel.

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:43.790
And we used the wind tunnel as part of our design process.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:52.060
At high speeds, wind can be extremely dangerous for a skyscraper.

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:55.160
Air rushes around the building.

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:58.160
forms mini-tornadoes called vortices.

00:33:59.270 --> 00:33:59.950
These areas.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:01.020
of low pressure.

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:02.870
suck the building sideways.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:07.110
And the taller the building, the more dangerous the vortices become.

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:11.910
And these large forces are actually perpendicular to the direction of the wind.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:14.520
If a tall building were ever to fall down the wind,

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:17.140
it's most likely it'd fall down sideways to the wind,

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:18.220
not in the direction of the wind.

00:34:21.050 --> 00:34:22.170
So on the Borge Dubai,

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.010
rather than fight the wind.

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:27.240
Bill and the design team decide to deceive it.

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:29.690
They don't make the tower flat.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:30.660
and rectangular.

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:34.760
that give the Burj Dubai a more unpredictable shape.

00:34:36.170 --> 00:34:40.710
Each section of the tower is designed to deflect the wind in a different way.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:44.140
This disrupts the power of the vortices.

00:34:44.620 --> 00:34:47.000
and breaks the hold of the wind on the building.

00:34:51.140 --> 00:34:56.240
As the wind blows across it, it never gets organized because every single portion of the building...

00:34:56.330 --> 00:34:58.830
will shed vortices at a different rate than the rest of the building.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:34:59.950
And we call this confusing.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:03.050
So we actually, when we designed the building,

00:35:03.190 --> 00:35:04.270
we're actually designing the wind

00:35:04.270 --> 00:35:07.090
and the way the wind behaves around the building.

00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:09.690
And it makes a tremendous difference.

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:11.410
We would never have been able to go this tall

00:35:11.410 --> 00:35:12.270
if we had not done that.

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:17.930
with mobility, gravity, heat, and wind conquered.

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:21.180
the skyscraper faces its next big challenge.

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:25.860
In Asia, where booming economies want to show off their wealth,

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:29.620
super-tall skyscrapers become the objects of desire.

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:34.180
But Asia is rife with the nemesis of tall buildings.

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:35.960
Earthquakes.

00:35:37.770 --> 00:35:40.810
to build the 509 meter Taipei 101.

00:35:41.480 --> 00:35:44.180
Skyscrapers take another leap forwards.

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:47.660
in 1999.

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:51.250
the architects of the world's tallest skyscraper

00:35:51.590 --> 00:35:53.170
to Taipei 101 in Taiwan.

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:54.550
face a problem.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:58.520
Taipei sits near the Pacific Ring of Fire.

00:35:58.950 --> 00:35:59.930
the most seismic

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:08.910
active area on Earth. An earthquake hits the city roughly twice a year. It's not a

00:36:08.910 --> 00:36:20.540
question of if but when the earth will shake the Taipei 101. Earthquakes are

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:25.340
actually really strong compared to wind. For example, wind loading very rarely

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:29.440
will break a large building but for an earthquake it's actually quite easy to

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:29.900
do with that.

00:36:31.210 --> 00:36:34.890
Adam Crew will test a model building on an earthquake simulator.

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:38.980
To make it realistic, this model is built with spaghetti.

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:42.780
Spaghetti and steel are actually quite similar in the way they behave.

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:45.600
You can actually see that it buckles quite nicely.

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:50.320
It's a relatively low load, and steel beams behave in a very, very similar way.

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:52.720
So it models steel really quite well.

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:57.100
With the top floor glued in place, the model is ready to face the tremors.

00:36:57.720 --> 00:36:59.980
Here comes the slow...

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:06.500
the first earthquake. You can see the building really isn't moving very much

00:37:06.500 --> 00:37:11.620
at all, looks pretty solid, so not really being bothered by this earthquake.

00:37:12.090 --> 00:37:17.130
There's a bit of a faster earthquake now, again a little bit of movement but the

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:28.940
building looks really nice and strong, even faster and it's gonna go, looks like the

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:29.100
building.

00:37:32.590 --> 00:37:36.870
If that had been a real building, the four would have fallen down and all the people would have been killed.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:41.970
So although it looks relatively intact, this is really a catastrophic failure for a building.

00:37:44.140 --> 00:37:49.500
Adam Crewe tests a second model that has elastic bands added to the spaghetti.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:54.540
So here comes the earthquake that smashed the other building. See how well this one does.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.550
it's it's moving a lot but it's actually performing really well top of the

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:08.750
building is actually hardly moving at all it's literally staying there

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:13.450
stationary and the ground is moving really quite violently underneath it so

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:17.880
slightly bizarrely by making the building more flexible you've made the

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:25.170
building stronger to survive in fast and violent quakes the Taipei 101 needs a

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:29.860
dash of elasticity, so the designers make their

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.300
building rigid where it has to be, but flexible where it can afford to be.

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:44.380
At the heart of the Taipei 101, they put 36 rigid steel tubes filled with concrete that

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:45.600
give the building strength.

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:52.730
While the columns stand firm during a quake, the rest of the structure is elastic, can flex

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:54.190
and roll with the punches.

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:05.760
Halfway during the construction, Mother Nature tests this design to the limit.

